  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLight rainfall continues across much of the area with continued\noverrunning. Flood risk in this activity is null. A southward\nmoving kink in the front has cleared our WV counties casting\nlarge questions as to whether or not we can even support the\nconvection required to get heavy rain back into our WV counties\nthis evening. This chance looks to be falling as we speak, with\nTucker the most likely culprit if any. A fractured, weak\nconvective line in central WV poses little threat to our\nnorthern WV counties but the area will continue to be monitored. \n\nLater this evening and into the overnight period, a wave of low\npressure riding along the main frontal boundary just to our\nsouth will reinvigorate showers especially to the south and east\nof Pittsburgh in the higher terrain, and perhaps even a\nthunderstorm or two in northern West Virginia with fumes of\nMUCAPE. Precipitable water remaining above 1.5 inches and decent\nwarm-cloud depths of 10-11kft will continue to support efficient\nrain rates. While a brief lull is possible late tonight into\nTuesday morning, additional rounds of showers are likely right\non through Tuesday night and into Wednesday at least, as an\nupper trough slowly digs toward the region from the Upper\nMidwest and potentially closes into an upper low Wednesday night\nor Thursday morning in our general vicinity.\n\n24-hour HREF mean precipitation totals from 8 PM tonight through\n8 PM Tuesday generally range from 1 to 1.25 inches down the\nspine of the ridges and through Morgantown. 90th percentile\ntotals are mostly between 1.5 and 2.5 inches, with a few 3+\nbullseyes illustrating the localized high rainfall potential.\nThe probability of 1\"+ hourly rates on the HREF through the\nperiod is quite low, but think this may be a bit underdone,\nespecially into Tuesday morning, given the convergence potential\nin the terrain and the still-high PWAT. Much of this area is\nalready quite wet, with 1-hour flash flood guidance values\naround or just below an inch. While the flash flooding threat\noverall may be decreasing especially after tonight, \noverflow/areal flooding threats remain. Thus, elected to extend\na good portion of the Flood Watch through 12Z Wednesday.\nCounties west of PIT/HLG were allowed to drop, as these areas\nare expected to avoid the higher rain rates/overall precip\ntotals. \n\nSupport for rain continues through Wednesday and into Thursday\nas the upper low develops, with higher totals backing a bit into\neastern Ohio - 0.50\" to 1.0 mean 24 hour totals through 8 PM \nWednesday according to the NBM. We won't lose the support for \ncontinued rain until likely Thursday when the upper low exits \nand surface high pressure centered over New England establishes \nand kicks the surface boundary out. There is still hope for a \ndrier pattern for Friday and next weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nCool temperatures are still figured for much of the week. Much\nof southwest PA/eastern Ohio north of the surface boundary has\nsunk into the 50s today, while 60s are maintained south of I-70.\nGiven the abundant cloud cover and stubborn east/northeast flow,\nhigher ridge locations may have trouble reaching 60 degrees \nTuesday through Thursday. Lower and mid 60s are still figured\nwest of the terrain, although lower values may again be possible\nin rain-cooled air. Minimum temperatures will remain fairly \nclose to normal given abundant moisture, but daytime highs are\nstill figured to run 10 degrees below normal through Thursday.",
